%S A087719 15,27,57,135,345,927,2577,7335,21225,62127,183297,543735,1618905,
%T A087719 4832127,14447217,43243335,129533385,388206927,1163834337,3489930135,
%U A087719 10466644665,31393642527,94168344657,282479868135,847389272745,2542067154927,7626000138177,22877597761335
%N A087719 Least number m such that the number of numbers k <= m with k > lpf(k)^n exceeds the number of numbers with k <= lpf(k)^n, where lpf = A020639.
%C A087719 m<a(n): #{k: k>lpf(k)^n & 1<=k<=m} <= m/2;
%C A087719 m>=a(n): #{k: k>lpf(k)^n & 1<=k<=m} > m/2.
%C A087719 a(n) = 3^n + 3*2^n + 6. Proof: Observe that for any composite k with lpf(k)>=5, we have k>=p^2. For the condition k>p^n to hold, k must be very large. No numbers with lpf >= 5 contribute to the "exceeding" count in the range 1...a(n). The count of satisfying numbers reduces to counting integers k from 1...m such that (2|k and k>2^n) or (3|k and k odd and k>3^n). This count can be expressed using floor functions: C(m) = floor(m/2) - 2^(n-1) + floor((m+3)/6) - floor((3^n+3)/6). The condition C(m) > m-C(m) leads to 2C(m) > m, and m = 3^n + 3*2^n + 6 is the smallest integer satisfying this inequality. - _Ralf Stephan_, Jan 27 2026
%H A087719 Andrew Howroyd, <a href="/A087719/b087719.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1000</a>
%H A087719 <a href="/index/Rec#order_03">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (6,-11,6).
%F A087719 a(n) = 3^n + 3*2^n + 6.
%F A087719 G.f.: 3*x*(5-21*x+20*x^2)/((1-x)*(1-2*x)*(1-3*x)). - _Colin Barker_, Feb 22 2012
%F A087719 Empirical: a(n) = 3*A183624(n-2), n>=3. - _R. J. Mathar_, Apr 16 2026
